Since $1\text{ megabyte}=1000\text{ kilobytes}$, we know that both $\dfrac{1\text{ megabyte}}{1000\text{ kilobytes}}$ and $\dfrac{1000\text{ kilobytes}}{1\text{ megabyte}}$ equal $1$.

So we can multiply by either of those fractions to help us convert units, since multiplying by $1$ doesn't change value. In this problem, we multiply by $\blueD{\dfrac{1\text{ megabyte}}{1000\text{ kilobytes}}}$ since the quantity we want to convert is in $\text{kilobytes}$:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}800\,\dfrac{\cancel{\text{kilobytes}}}{\text{second}}\cdot \blueD{\dfrac{1\text{ megabyte}}{1000\,\,\cancel{\text{kilobytes}}}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{800}{1000}\,\dfrac{\text{megabytes}}{\text{second}}
\end{align}$
